WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:11.680
Today we are going to explore what happens&nbsp;
if we unleash an AI to read the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.200
and then, ask it some silly questions and&nbsp;
we’ll get some really amazing answers.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:27.280
But how? Well, in the last few years, OpenAI set&nbsp;
out to train an AI named GPT-3 that could finish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:36.320
your sentences. Then, they made Image-GPT, this&nbsp;
could even finish your images. Yes, not kidding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:43.760
It could identify that the cat here likely holds a&nbsp;
piece of paper and finish the picture accordingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:50.640
and even understood that if we have a droplet&nbsp;
here and we see just a portion of the ripples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.520
then this means a splash must be filled in.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:01.440
So, in summary, GPT-3 is trained to&nbsp;
finish our sentences, or even your images.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:09.680
However, scientists at OpenAI identified that&nbsp;
it really isn’t great at following instructions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:16.640
Here is an excellent example. Look, we ask it&nbsp;
to explain the moon landing to a 6-year old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:23.360
it gets very confused. It seems to&nbsp;
be in text completion mode instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.480
trying to follow our instructions.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, this is InstructGPT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:36.400
their new method, which can not only finish our&nbsp;
sentences, but also, follow our instructions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:43.600
And, would you look at that! It does&nbsp;
that successfully. Good job, little AI.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.160
This was a really simple example. But of&nbsp;
course, we are experienced Fellow Scholars here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:57.200
so let’s try to find out what this AI is really&nbsp;
capable of in three really cool examples.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.360
Remember in The Hitchhiker's Guide&nbsp;
To The&nbsp;Galaxy where people could ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.880
an all-knowing machine the most important&nbsp;
questions of things that trouble humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:14.400
Yes! We are going to do exactly that.&nbsp;
Round 1. So, dear all-knowing AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.240
what happens if you fire a cannonball&nbsp;
directly at a pumpkin at high speeds?

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:28.720
Well. What? Do you see that? According&nbsp;
to GPT-3, pumpkins are strong magnets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.920
I don’t know which internet&nbsp;
forum told the AI that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:38.960
Not good. Now, hold on to your papers, and&nbsp;
let’s see the new technique’s answer together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:47.760
Wow, this is so much more informative. Let’s&nbsp;
break it down together. It starts out by hedging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.840
noting that is it hard to say there are&nbsp;
too many unpredictable factors involved.&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:01.680
Annoying as it might seem, it is correct to&nbsp;
say all this. Good start. Then, it lists some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:07.360
of the factors that might decide the fate of&nbsp;
that pumpkin, like the size of the cannonball,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.080
distance and velocity, yes, we’re getting&nbsp;
there, but please, give me something concrete.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:22.480
Yes, there we go! It says that, quote “Some of the&nbsp;
more likely possible outcomes include breaking or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.400
knocking the pumpkin to the ground, cracking&nbsp;
the pumpkin, or completely obliterating it.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.040
Excellent. A little smartypants AI&nbsp;
at our disposal. Amazing. I love it.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:41.920
Round 2, code summarization. While&nbsp;
DeepMind’s AlphaCode is capable of reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:49.040
a competition-level programming problem and coding&nbsp;
up a correct solution right in front of our eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:58.240
That is all well and good, but if we give GPT-3&nbsp;
a piece of code and ask it what it does, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:06.640
the answer is not only not very informative,&nbsp;
but it’s also incorrect. InstructGPT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.560
gives a much more insightful answer which shows&nbsp;
a bit of understanding of what this code does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.920
That is amazing. Note that it&nbsp;
is still not completely right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.440
but it is directionally correct. Partial credit.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:29.280
Round 3, write me a poem. About&nbsp;
what? Well, about a wise frog.&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:37.200
With GPT-3, we get the usual confusion. By round&nbsp;
3 we really see that it was really not meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:47.760
do this. And, with InstructGPT, let’s see. Hmm…an&nbsp;
all-knowing frog, who is the master of disguise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.200
a great teacher, and quite possibly&nbsp;
the bringer of peace and tranquility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:01.680
to humanity. All written by the AI. This is&nbsp;
fantastic. I love it. What a time to be alive!

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.040
Now, we only looked at 3 examples here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:12.800
but what about the rest? Worry not for a second,&nbsp;
OpenAI’s scientists ran a detailed user study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:19.600
and found out that people preferred InstructGPTs&nbsp;
solutions way more often than the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:26.240
techniques on a larger set of questions. That&nbsp;
is a huge difference. Absolutely amazing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.360
Once again, incredible progress&nbsp;
just one more paper down the line.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:39.920
So, is it perfect? No, of course not, so let’s&nbsp;
highlight one of its limitations. If the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:47.360
contains false premises, it accepts the premise&nbsp;
as being real, and goes with it. This leads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:57.360
making things up. Yes, really. Check this out.
Why aren’t birds real? GPT-3 says….something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:05.280
I am not sure what this one is about. This almost&nbsp;
sounds like gibberish. While, InstructGPT accepts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:13.360
the fact that birds aren’t real, and even helps us&nbsp;
craft an argument for that. This is a limitation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:20.080
and I must say, a quite remarkable one. An&nbsp;
AI that makes things up. Food for thought.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.480
And, remember, at the start of this episode,&nbsp;
also looked at this moon landing example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:32.320
Did you notice the issue there? Please&nbsp;
let me know in the comments below! So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:38.640
what is the issue here? Well, beyond not being&nbsp;
all that informative, it was asked to describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:46.960
the moon landing in a few sentences. This is not&nbsp;
a few sentences. This is one sentence. If we give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:53.120
it constraints like that, it tries to adhere&nbsp;
to them, but is often not too great at that.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:00.320
And, of course, both of these shortcomings show&nbsp;
us the way for an even better followup paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:07.280
which, based on previous progress in AI&nbsp;
research, could appear maybe not even in years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:12.480
but much quicker than that. If you are&nbsp;
interested in such a followup work, make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:18.160
sure to subscribe and hit the bell icon to not&nbsp;
miss it when it appears here on Two Minute Papers.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:24.640
So, what would you use this for? What do you&nbsp;
expect to happen a couple more papers down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.040
line? Please let me know in the comments&nbsp;
below. I’d love to hear your thoughts.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:23.520
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

